WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Film Courage: Brian we hear filmmakers say that
filmmaking keeps them out of trouble

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:08.340
or they say "You know this is all I can
do. This is why I do it even though it's

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.080
hard at times." Sometimes I wonder is it
really all they can do? Or that's just

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.350
what they sort of say to themselves
that's what they want to do. What are

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:22.830
your thoughts on this? Brian Avenet-Bradley, Horror Filmmaker: I'm sure it varies
for each filmmaker I mean for me I can

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:28.410
do other things but what I find happens
is if I don't do something creative and

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:33.329
for me my creativity teams always go
visual so it always goes into movies if

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:36.870
a certain amount of time passes and I
haven't done something I start to go a

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.980
little crazy a little stir-crazy
so there's like this drive - for me it's

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.210
a drive to tell stories and then
eventually and I write a lot and I write

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.980
a lot of stories and usually the story
we end up doing with my filmmaking

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.780
partner Lo is usually one that I've had
for a while that I keep coming back to

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.230
or won't go away like you won't
sometimes I'll be able to write

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.710
something it's kind of done even though
it's like never see the light of day

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.820
maybe it's a script or a story or just
an idea I can get it out of my system

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.150
and it feels like okay I'm okay but
every now and then there's a story that

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.900
they just keep that keep thinking about
it it comes back and comes back and

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.720
comes back and usually that's the one
it's like I have to do this one you know

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.150
find a way to make it in a format that
other people can see it you know is

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.680
there sort of a timeframe for you like
if you're in between projects is there

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.880
like a three-month window where if you
don't have something new to work on you

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.490
can start to feel yourself slipping so
to speak I mean I'm always like thinking

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:40.860
of ideas so it's like the same flaw
greater than three month window for me

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.640
as long as that as long as I'm like
collecting ideas and thinking about

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.259
stories and writing it down I'm fine I
think it's like if if it's like going up

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:54.570
to a year and are not actively involved
in making a movie or making some other

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.320
creative project which will come to
fruition so other people can see it then

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.610
I probably get get the shakes unless
it's like I have to do it you know I

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:07.799
think something uh-huh yeah what do you
start to see yourself slipping into when

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:12.150
you know like you you become bored
easily or how do you know like oh you

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.810
know what this is after a while you
learn

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.500
this is because I need to start working
on something you know for me it's like

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.580
really I just kind of get into a malaise
it's like is it really it's like doing

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.959
something creative is kind of what makes
me feel alive and if too much time goes

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:29.890
by and I'm not doing something creative
then it's just I don't know I find

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:36.250
myself kind of sinking into this like
malaise of just life isn't really

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.280
working for me and then I'm like what's
wrong and I'm like it kind of hits me

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.870
like Oh cuz I'm not doing something
great it's amazingly I forget every time

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.800
you know like why am I feeling this way
and then it's like oh excuse I'm not

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.000
creatively involved in something
fortunately I have a lot of other things

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.920
that I do which are also creative so
that helps me kind of sustaining a

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.989
little bit but for me it's like the
projects I'm most passionate about are

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:05.890
the ones that my wife and filmmaking
partner Laurence Avenet-Bradley ("Lo") that we do

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:10.959
together and those are the ones because
it's our project and it's it's a passion

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:16.000
project so we kind of control it from
beginning middle and end so it's very

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.600
much like our vision of what we want to
tell and that is to me the most

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.680
creatively satisfying is when you can do
a project like that where it's not going

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.790
to have too much outside influence that
changes what you're trying to make what

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.769
other creative things do you do oh I do
editing so I do editing for a television

00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:37.780
series television shows which obviously
involves a lot of creativity I do a lot

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:44.560
of like docu reality type shows which
actually requires you as an editor to be

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.850
very involved in story because you're
working with the story people and the

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.989
producers to help figure out the story
and develop so it's like it's more than

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:58.000
just cutting something from a script
it's very it really engages you

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.299
creatively because you're really working
on figuring out like well how do we tell

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:04.329
the story what is the story
sometimes you're figuring out well you

00:04:04.329 --> 00:04:07.239
know what is this you know what is the
story and how do you tell a beginning

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:12.670
middle and end and make it dramatic and
keep people engaged with it it heard an

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.959
author speaker this weekend a Brazilian
author and forgive me on her name I'll

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.350
put it at the below-the-line part of the
video but she said that when she gets

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:24.280
writer's block and she says it's very
real that she has to do things like go

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.759
to museums
just just you know the other creative

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:32.620
things that are not writing do you ever
feel blocked and if so do you have a

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:38.289
ways to get out of it well I definitely
feel like going places like Lo and I

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:42.219
like taking trips and going places we
like to go out to the desert or to the

00:04:42.219 --> 00:04:46.629
mountains sometimes up to the beach or
the ocean it's like I get a lot of

00:04:46.629 --> 00:04:53.020
inspiration from locations especially on
with movies and it gets a little tricky

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.529
sometimes because sometimes I fall so in
love with the location it's hard for me

00:04:56.529 --> 00:05:00.759
to imagine not shooting it there but but
they'll be usually a location can be a

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:05.919
really big inspiration for me like I'll
see something and then it starts my mind

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:10.930
going what if what if what if and
there's been places that we've

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:17.229
incorporated in our films sometimes it's
not that exact same location but like an

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:21.639
idea from that location will find its
way into the story something inspired

00:05:21.639 --> 00:05:27.699
mice so I find like the best way for me
at least creatively is to find the

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:33.099
inspiration from real life because if
you don't do that then I feel like you

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:37.569
get into a cycle of copying which is
really easy to fall into you mean it's

00:05:37.569 --> 00:05:42.310
really easy to look at a movie and go oh
I like that movie but I would have done

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.460
something like this with that movie and
then you know you can easily change it a

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:51.399
few degrees and it'd be a new movie but
in essence it's gonna be so built on the

00:05:51.399 --> 00:05:55.000
other film that it's not really a new
thing whereas if you can get the

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.259
inspiration from life or or stories you
hear from other people like real stories

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:04.569
that happen to them or they know of a
friend something happened a lot of times

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:08.529
that can spark the imagination into
thinking like oh well you know what if

00:06:08.529 --> 00:06:12.430
what if that story happened and then
this happened and that happened and to

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:16.330
me that's that's the most fun it's kind
of like taking the inspiration from real

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:24.039
life is location of our character and if
you're filming I'm sure yeah yes as I

00:06:24.039 --> 00:06:30.909
would say location for us is almost
always a character and almost every film

00:06:30.909 --> 00:06:39.030
we've done I think it's funny cuz this
film dark remains I forget this is the

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:45.940
this is our dark remains a location that
Lawrence lo found as I was writing the

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.139
script she fell in love with this like
prison location that was in Rome Gona

00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:55.240
which is no longer in existence but it
was a closed prison but they we could

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.720
actually go there and shoot and she's
like well you have to add a prison to

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.720
the story because it's prison so great
I'm like well there's no way to put a

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.169
prison in the story anyway after like
complaining for a while I woke up the

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:08.979
next day and I'm like oh well if this
prison became part of the story like

00:07:08.979 --> 00:07:13.120
this it changed everything it made it
better I mean it rippled through and the

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.110
prison became such a thing like when it
came out in Japan they're like they they

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:21.669
retitled a ghost prison like you know
none of these things are in the movie

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:24.849
but is they even the prison it's in the
movie and but they like to do this with

00:07:24.849 --> 00:07:28.719
clip art and change the graphics when it
goes overseas but they decided they

00:07:28.719 --> 00:07:32.979
loved the prison so much the movie title
became ghost prison so definitely like

00:07:32.979 --> 00:07:39.490
locations become like a huge thing this
film which was our second film goes to

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:45.610
the needle which they reach out at 6
eeeh I think in Spain that one factor a

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:49.479
factory is a very key location for the
main character and a lot of events take

00:07:49.479 --> 00:07:54.310
place in the factory and that became
like a very key part to the film and

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:59.830
once we found the factory we did a lot
of revisions on the script in writing

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:03.550
because the the location inspired us
because I had other things in the script

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.490
but once you see the real Factory you're
like oh well this could happen this

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:09.490
could happen and this is there was an
elevator there there's this amazing

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:13.180
freight elevator that was there and that
added like this whole thing with going

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:17.889
between the different floors and and so
I'm like for me it's like locations

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:24.370
including the first one we did freezer
was our first film and this actually

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:29.770
took place on what was the family farm
they spent a lot of time on growing up I

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:35.890
was in Vergania right across the border
from Tinesy and and I spent a lot of

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:41.260
childhood years and weekends and weeks
on this farm and that location was like

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.100
it basically was the script because they
basically once I came up with a story

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:49.839
and realized that what's happening here
it like you would go with the characters

00:08:49.839 --> 00:08:52.430
here and the characters in the
situation's what would happen

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.339
because there are these things in that
location so into bringing your

00:08:55.339 --> 00:09:00.770
protagonist your antagonists and you go
what would they do and then based on

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:05.089
being in this environment and then you
basically get scenes and you get moments

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:09.380
and it actually influences the writing
of the script and I find that I don't

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:14.420
know I find that very exciting when your
location can actually be the look when

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.720
the location that inspire is the story
can actually be the one that you shoot

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:22.130
at that's the best I mean it doesn't
always worked out because you maybe

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.810
can't for practical reasons you did that
location but that's the best case

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:31.130
scenario otherwise whenever you do get
the real location you you kind of have

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.850
to embrace it and make changes instead
of trying to fight it to be the location

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.510
you don't have because that'll work
against you but location definitely a

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.890
character yeah

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.420
you

